**YES \(L[2/5]\), conditional on the stated internal chain; the growing-degree XK step introduces no larger exponent.**

With identical parameters for the outer DL algorithm and its Theorem U factoring subroutine, the resulting cost constant equals Theorem U’s stated constant. This establishes a bound for computing one logarithm. A cheaper subsequent-logarithm bound does not follow from the supplied relation-supply statements.

I respected the blindness restrictions.

**1. Algorithm and theorem**

Write
\[
\lambda=\log\log p,\qquad D=\frac{\log p}{\lambda},\qquad
\Lambda=D^{2/5}\lambda,\qquad r=2^{-2/5}.
\]
Here \(L_p[2/5,c]=\exp(c\Lambda)\), and every \(o(1)\) below is relative to \(\Lambda\).

Fix positive rationals \(a,\kappa,d_0\). Use U’s parameters:
\[
\begin{aligned}
d&=\text{least odd integer }\ge\max(3,d_0D^{1/5}),\\
k&=\max(\lceil\kappa D^{2/5}\rceil,d+1),\qquad \nu=\frac1{4k},\\
\eta_0&=\frac1{\lceil D^{1/2}\rceil},\qquad B=\lfloor e^{a\Lambda}\rfloor,\\
\mu&=
\begin{cases}
(1-\nu)/d,&k<(d^2+1)/4,\\
(1+(d-1)\nu)/(d+1),&k\ge(d^2+1)/4,
\end{cases}\\
h&=\max(2\nu,\mu-(d-2)\nu,1-d\mu+\nu)+\eta_0.
\end{aligned}
\]
Put \(M=\lfloor p^\mu\rfloor\), \(H=\lfloor p^h\rfloor\), and
\(W=\lceil p^{\mu+\nu+\eta_0/2}\rceil\).

The remaining parameters are exactly U’s:

- \(X=\lfloor\sqrt{p/2}\rfloor\); \(j\) maximal with \(2^{kj}\le X\); \(Y=2^{j-1}\); \(J=(Y,2Y]\cap\mathbb P\).
- Common radius \(V(S)=\lfloor\sqrt{2Y}\,S\rfloor\).
- Candidates have prime \(b\in((1-1/d)V,V]\), \(|a|\le V\), and \(\gcd(a,b)=1\).
- \(S\) is found by U’s Rankin-value scan, evaluated at the actual
  \[
  X_r(S)=2MV(S)/Y,\qquad X_F(S)=(2d+1)MHV(S)^d.
  \]
- Fields satisfy \(F(g_0,g_1)=pw\), with U’s field law and rejection tests.

These choices are specified in [U’s parameter section](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/lv5unif_results.md:103).

For a primitive root \(g\), target \(h\), and \(n=p-1\), run the following algorithm.

1. Select a field by retaining the first field whose descent attempt returns a row.
2. Each attempt independently draws \(a_i,b_i\) uniformly modulo \(n\), forms
   \[
   T_i=g^{a_i}h^{b_i},
   \]
   and Gauss-splits \(T_i\equiv u_0/v_0\pmod p\).
3. Completely factor \(|u_0|,|v_0|\le\sqrt{2p}\) using **Theorem U**. Accept the shape only when both sides are products of at most \(k\) distinct primes in \(J\). Descend every prime through U’s special-\(q\) search.
4. Collect
   \[
   m_{\rm rows}=\bar C+d,\qquad
   \bar C=(d+1)\pi(B)+1
   \]
   rows. Construct XK’s exact integer exponent matrix.
5. Apply the growing-degree XK procedure proved below. Obtain a primitive \(x\in\mathbb Z^{m_{\rm rows}}\) with
   \[
   W_x=\prod_iT_i^{x_i}\in\{1,-1\}.
   \]
   Its known logarithm is \(\ell_x=0\) or \(n/2\).
6. Set \(A=\sum x_i a_i\), \(B_*=\sum x_i b_i\pmod n\). If \(\gcd(B_*,n)=1\), return
   \[
   X=(\ell_x-A)B_*^{-1}\pmod n
   \]
   after verifying \(g^X=h\). Otherwise discard the round’s rows and draw fresh ones in the retained field.

Small inputs can be handled by an interleaved exhaustive DL algorithm, so no unknown asymptotic threshold needs to be tested.

**Theorem DL25, as I would state it.** Assume the stipulated uniform internal chain of Theorem U, including its validity for the raw relation construction at prime \(N\). Suppose the complete factoring subroutine has uniform expected cost
\[
F(x)\le L_x[2/5,c_F+o(1)].
\]
Then the algorithm above computes \(\log_g h\) for every prime \(p\), primitive root \(g\), and \(h\ne0\), with expected bit complexity
\[
L_p[2/5,c_{\rm DL}+o(1)],
\]
where
\[
\boxed{
c_{\rm DL}
=\max\left\{
3a,\;
a+s+\max(a,\sigma,r c_F)
\right\},
}
\tag{1}
\]
and
\[
s=2\kappa(1+C_\gamma),\qquad
\sigma=\frac{2\theta_0}{5a},\qquad
\theta_0=
\begin{cases}
3/d_0,&\kappa\ge d_0^2/4,\\
2/d_0+d_0/(4\kappa),&\kappa<d_0^2/4.
\end{cases}
\tag{2}
\]
Here \(C_\gamma=C_\gamma(a,\kappa,d_0)<\infty\) is U’s exponent in
\(\gamma\ge(\log p)^{-C_\gamma}\).

There is no additional conjecture concerning units, class groups, Schirokauer maps, matrix rank, or the factorization of \(p-1\). The arbitrary-base version, promised \(h\in\langle g\rangle\), follows by the existing auxiliary-base and verified-restart reduction, with the same constant.

**2. Ingredient audit**

“Verbatim” means U’s repaired, uniform statement—not substitution into an estimate containing an uncontrolled \(O_d\).

| Ingredient | Status | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Field construction, GB, sizes | Transfers verbatim | \(F(g_0,g_1)=pw\) gives the required root modulo \(p\); no compositeness is needed. |
| FM-U | Transfers verbatim | Its arithmetic requirement is the reduced moving residue, particularly \((p,t)=1\). Here \(0<t\le2MV<p\). |
| Lemma \(8.5'(R')\) | Transfers verbatim | The smooth-number and modulus parameters are unchanged. The ambient integer enters through sizes and reduced residues. |
| Kern-U | Transfers verbatim | An integer fibre-counting statement; independent of the arithmetic of \(p\). |
| W-U, DS-U, L2-U, LD*-U | Transfer verbatim | The multiplier law, common radius, narrow \(b\)-window, and height margins are unchanged. |
| Lemma CS | Transfers verbatim | Same computable saddle-point scan, including the \(S^d\) contribution to \(X_F\). |
| D-U | Transfers verbatim | Same moments and Paley–Zygmund argument give the same polylogarithmic \(\gamma\). |
| Lemma S″ | Replaced | Uniform targets range over all of \(\mathbb F_p^\times\); use the coprime-pair injection below. |
| K-U | Modified | Same shape event and special-\(q\) recognition; use the new split lemma. The leading split cost is unchanged. |
| select-U | Modified | Replace expected rounds \(\le2\) by \(n/\varphi(n)\). Keep its sharper treatment of post-processing. |
| Field rejections | Transfer | Irreducibility, content, leading coefficient, and simple-root tests remain necessary. Their rejection mass is \(o(\gamma)\). |
| Outer factor exits | Disappear | A gcd with prime \(p\) cannot return a proper factor. A zero residue causes rejection where prescribed. |
| LV6 and DD | Replaced | Factoring fruitfulness fails at prime modulus: DD gives success probability zero. XK and hidden exponents supply DL extraction instead. |
| Recursive shape test | Used as a factoring subroutine | Inner inputs are ordinary integers, so Theorem U applies directly. |

The relevant arithmetic checks are stronger asymptotically than at fixed degree:
\[
2MV,\quad X_F,\quad |f_d|,\quad w<p
\]
for sufficiently large \(p\). In particular, the nonzero rational and algebraic values used in rows are units modulo \(p\).

Since \(\|f\|_\infty<p\) and its content divides \(pw\), retained content is \(1\); the \(e=w\) case is rejected. The selected root \(m=g_0/g_1\) must remain simple. **It is unnecessary to require \(p\nmid\operatorname{disc}f\) globally**: other roots may be multiple while the selected root still defines the required degree-one prime.

No outer step uses \(\omega(N)\ge2\), after DD is removed. Inner factoring calls retain U’s usual prime tests and factor exits.

**The replacement for S″.** Let \(\mathcal G\subset J\), \(m=|\mathcal G|\ge4k^3\), and let \(\mathcal S\) contain products of \(k\) distinct primes of \(\mathcal G\) in \([Z/2,Z]\), where
\[
|\mathcal S|\ge\binom mk/(2k),\qquad 2Z^2<p.
\]
For each \(u\in\mathcal S\), the number of \(v\in\mathcal S\) sharing a prime with \(u\) is at most
\[
k\binom{m-1}{k-1}
\le \frac{2k^3}{m}|\mathcal S|.
\]
Thus at least \(|\mathcal S|^2/2\) ordered pairs are coprime.

The map
\[
(u,v)\longmapsto uv^{-1}\pmod p
\]
is injective on these pairs: equality modulo \(p\) implies
\(uv'-u'v=0\), because its absolute value is below \(p\), and reduced positive fractions are unique.

Moreover, any shortest lattice vector for this target is \(\pm(u,v)\). Its determinant with \((u,v)\) is a multiple of \(p\) of absolute value at most \(2Z^2<p\), hence zero; coprimality then identifies the vector. Consequently,
\[
\boxed{\Pr_T[\text{accepted split}]\ge
\frac{|\mathcal S|^2}{2(p-1)}.}
\tag{3}
\]
This proves the needed growing-\(k\) statement without square classes, a central binomial coefficient, or a second-moment multiplicity factor.

It yields U’s usable lower bound
\[
q_{\rm sel}\ge
\frac{\gamma}{4}\,
\frac{(\gamma/(16\log p))^{2k}}{32k^2},
\qquad
q_{\rm sel}^{-1}\le \exp((s+o(1))\Lambda).
\tag{4}
\]

**3. Shape factoring, recursion, and the cost constant**

The outer DL algorithm is **non-recursive**. It calls an already analysed factoring algorithm on smaller integers:
\[
F(\sqrt{2p})\le L_p[2/5,r c_F+o(1)].
\]
The fixpoint belongs to the proof of Theorem U. There is no second DL fixpoint.

Logging \(u_0\) or \(v_0\) would leave the computation in \(\mathbb F_p^\times\). Their small integer representatives do not give smaller DL groups, nor do their logarithms certify the required prime-product shape.

A descent attempt has expected cost at most
\[
L_p[2/5,t+o(1)],\qquad
t=\max(r c_F,a,\sigma),
\]
because U’s special-\(q\) search costs \(\widetilde O(B+S^2)\), with
\(S^2=L_p[2/5,\sigma+o(1)]\).

Field selection gives the retained field the usual size-biased distribution. In particular,
\[
\mathbb E[1/q_A\mid A\text{ retained}]\le1/q_{\rm sel}.
\]
The \(m_{\rm rows}=L_p[2/5,a+o(1)]\) rows therefore cost
\(L_p[2/5,a+s+t+o(1)]\). XK costs \(L_p[2/5,3a+o(1)]\).

The number of rounds contributes only
\[
n/\varphi(n)=\exp(o(\Lambda)).
\]
Post-processing is multiplied by this round factor, **not** by \(q_{\rm sel}^{-1}\). This proves (1).

For comparison, [Theorem U](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/lv5unif_results.md:614) gives
\[
c_U=\max\left\{
2a,\;
P,\;
\frac{\delta}{1-r}
\right\},
\quad
P=a+s+\max(a,\sigma),\quad \delta=a+s.
\]
Here \(P\ge2a\), and
\[
c_U=\max(P,\delta+r c_U).
\]
Also
\[
c_U\ge\frac{a+s}{1-r}>
\frac a{1-r}>4.1298a>3a.
\]
Therefore, using U with the **same parameter tuple**,
\[
\boxed{c_{\rm DL}=c_U.}
\tag{5}
\]

Thus cubic linear algebra is strictly slack throughout this inherited family, including its optimum. The fixed-degree statement that “\(3a\) takes over” in some \(L[1/2]\) regimes does not apply here.

For a concrete finite choice, take
\((a,\kappa,d_0)=(3/5,1/8,1)\). Then \(\theta_0=4\), \(\sigma=8/3\), and
\[
c_{\rm DL}=c_U=
\max\left\{
\frac{49}{15}+\frac{1+C_\gamma}{4},\;
\frac{\frac35+\frac{1+C_\gamma}{4}}{1-2^{-2/5}}
\right\}<\infty.
\]
This is an explicit formula, not a certified numerical constant: the actual \(C_\gamma\) has not been numerically bounded.

**4. Proposition XK at growing degree**

The fixed-degree notation \(\widetilde O_d\) alone would not justify this transfer. The following bounds do.

**Exact ideals and local orders.** Put \(t=f_d\), \(\alpha\) a root, and
\[
\mathfrak a=(1,\alpha),\qquad
\mathfrak J_P=(a-b\alpha)\mathfrak a^{-1}.
\]
The ideals \(\mathfrak J_P\) are integral and have norm \(|F(P)|\). Include all occurring prime ideals above rational primes at most \(B\), including those above bad primes. There are at most \(d\pi(B)\) such columns.

At each known bad prime \(r\le B\), compute an \(r\)-maximal order and the required valuations. This is polynomial in the field’s input size and \(\log r\); it does not require factoring the large part of the discriminant. Buchmann–Lenstra’s Theorem 1.2 and Algorithm 6.1/Proposition 6.2 explicitly provide the local maximal-order algorithm. I read those statements and the polynomial-time argument. [Buchmann–Lenstra, 1994, pp. 225, 246–247](https://www.numdam.org/item/JTNB_1994__6_2_221_0.pdf).

The \(g_1\) column enforces zero total atom exponent, thereby cancelling the common fractional ideal \(\mathfrak a\). This also handles its possible support above primes larger than \(B\).

**Integer solving and coefficient sizes.** Matrix entries and row support sizes are polynomial in \(d,k,\log p\), hence polynomial in \(\log p\). If the rational rank is \(\rho\), Hadamard gives kernel-vector entry lengths
\[
\beta_x=O(\rho\log\log p)
=L_p[2/5,a+o(1)].
\]
Compute a modular rank profile, solve only \(d\) right-hand sides, and verify the resulting integer dependencies on every column. A rank-preserving prime guarantees success with constant probability. Verification need not certify the full rational rank: the selected dependencies are independent through their separate free coordinates.

Dixon lifting has cubic cost in \(\rho\), up to factors polynomial in \(d,\log p,\log\rho\), rather than elimination on large rational fractions. Its lifting and rational-reconstruction bounds therefore give
\[
L_p[2/5,3a+o(1)].
\]
I checked Dixon’s original argument in a text reproduction, including the dependence on entry bounds. [Dixon, *Numerische Mathematik* 40 (1982), 137–141](https://paperzz.com/doc/8594966/exact-solution-of-linear-equations-using).

**Conjugate lower bounds and precision.** Cauchy’s root bound gives
\[
|\sigma(a-b\alpha)|\le U,\qquad \log U=O(\log p).
\]
Since
\[
|N_{K/\mathbb Q}(a-b\alpha)|=|F(P)|/|t|\ge1/|t|,
\]
every conjugate satisfies
\[
|\sigma(a-b\alpha)|\ge |t|^{-1}U^{-(d-1)}.
\]
Thus atom logarithms have magnitude polynomial in \(d,\log p\). Converting absolute root precision into logarithm precision loses only \(O(d\log p)\) additional bits.

Certified root isolation requires polynomial work in \(d\) and the coefficient bit length; high-precision refinement can then be performed with cost nearly linear in the requested precision. There is no need to construct a huge product \(\gamma_x\) explicitly. [Pan, *Journal of Symbolic Computation* 33 (2002), 701–733](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0747717102905316).

**The gap and small relations.** The supplied Blanksby–Montgomery bound gives the explicit unit-log gap
\[
\beta_d=\frac1d\log\left(1+\frac1{52d\log(6d)}\right)
\asymp\frac1{d^2\log d}.
\]
For \(s=r_U+1\le d\) independent exact kernel vectors, put
\(v_j=\operatorname{Log}\gamma_{x_j}\). The LLL-basis argument in XK bounds a nonzero integer relation by
\[
R\le
\max\left(1,\,
d!\left(\frac{2^{O(d^2)}\max_j\|v_j\|}{\beta_d}\right)^d
\right).
\]
Consequently,
\[
\log R=
O\!\left(
d\beta_x+d\log m_{\rm rows}
+d\log(kd\log p)+d^3
\right).
\tag{6}
\]
The \(d!\) and \(2^{O(d^2)}\) factors are harmless here because the algorithm uses **\(\log R\)** to choose precision. It does not enumerate vectors up to \(R\).

Taking the scaled-log lattice precision above \(\log R\), with the usual LLL approximation-factor margin, forces its short vector to be a true unit-log relation. Evaluate atom logs to a further
\[
\beta_x+\log(2k m_{\rm rows})+O(d\log p)
\]
bits to control weighted accumulation and rounding. The total required precision is therefore
\[
P=L_p[2/5,a+o(1)].
\]

LLL acts on a lattice of rank at most \(d\), in ambient dimension at most \(2d\). A proven quadratic-in-bit-length implementation costs
\[
\operatorname{poly}(d)\,\widetilde O(P^2)
=L_p[2/5,2a+o(1)].
\]
Nguyen–Stehlé give precisely such polynomial dimension dependence. [*SIAM Journal on Computing* 39 (2009), 874–903](https://epubs.siam.org/doi/abs/10.1137/070705702).

Log evaluation, exact verification, and final modular exponentiation likewise cost at most \(L_p[2/5,2a+o(1)]\). In particular,
\[
d^2=o(\Lambda),\qquad d\log d=o(\Lambda).
\]
Even an actual \(2^{O(d^2)}\) runtime multiplier would be \(L_p[2/5,o(1)]\). No actual \(2^{O(d^3)}\) runtime is incurred by (6).

Finally, odd \(d\) implies \(\mu_K=\{\pm1\}\). The torsion-relation lattice is saturated in \(\mathbb Z^{m_{\rm rows}}\), so dividing the returned vector by its coordinate gcd preserves the torsion property. This supplies the primitive vector required for extraction.

Hence the uniform extension of [Proposition XK](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/dlog_results.md:260) has total cost
\[
\boxed{T_{\rm XK}=L_p[2/5,3a+o(1)].}
\]

**5. Randomization, recursive coins, and individual logarithms**

For \(X=\log_g h\), the map
\[
(a,b)\longmapsto(a+Xb,b)
\]
is a bijection modulo \(n\). Thus \(T=g^ah^b\) is uniform, and \(b\) is uniform conditional on \(T\).

Every operation before extraction—including field selection, shape factoring, special-\(q\) caching, exact solving, and unit elimination—must depend only on the targets and coins independent of their labels. The shape-factorization calls receive only the integer halves and fresh coins. Their sorted prime-factor outputs are canonical; their random durations do not reveal information about \(b\) conditional on \(T\).

For each round, condition on its targets, all its independent coins, and the complete history of earlier rounds. The current \(b_i\) remain independent uniform residues. Because \(x\) is primitive,
\[
\sum_i x_i b_i
\]
is uniform modulo \(n\). The extraction probability is exactly
\(\varphi(n)/n\).

This conditioning is deliberately **per round**. Conditioning on the current round’s final gcd outcome would destroy that uniformity statement.

The algorithm already performs individual-target descent: every row is a descended randomized target. However, it needs about \(B\) such rows for each requested logarithm.

A target-independent field can be selected beforehand using uniform powers of \(g\). Its setup cost is bounded by
\[
L_p[2/5,s+t+o(1)].
\]
That field, prime tables, and caches can be reused. The proven per-log bound remains (1), averaged over this preprocessing.

A Pomerance-style cheaper per-log phase needs more. Precomputed rows must span the later target rows in a suitable saturated module, with useful coefficients modulo the unfactored \(p-1\). The supplied chain proves neither property. An exact primitive dependency after adjoining a target could use only precomputed rows; rational spanning alone can also introduce a denominator sharing a factor with \(p-1\).

Thus **the full-run bound per logarithm is justified; a strictly cheaper per-log bound is not established here**. This is a limitation of the available proof, not an impossibility result for preprocessing.

**Weakest link and limits of the conclusion**

The decisive inherited input remains
\[
\gamma\ge(\log p)^{-C_\gamma}
\]
uniformly in growing degree, after field rejections. It is raised to approximately \(2k\). Replacing that precision by an unspecified \(L_p[2/5,-o(1)]\) would not suffice.

Under the stipulated \(\tau=0\) chain, I found no additional obstruction. The new bookkeeping requiring care is the explicit precision estimate (6), rather than reliance on fixed-degree \(\widetilde O_d\) notation.

This one-level design does not justify \(\alpha<2/5\). Its size constraints give
\[
k=O(D^\alpha),\qquad
\theta=O(D^{2\alpha-1}),\qquad
\theta\gtrsim1/d,\qquad \theta\gtrsim d/k,
\]
hence \(k\gtrsim D^{2-4\alpha}\), forcing \(\alpha\ge2/5\). This is a restriction of the analysed design.

**6. Literature and extension fields**

The literature conclusions below are separate from the conditional internal theorem.

| Setting | Bound/status | Source and reading scope |
|---|---|---|
| Every prime field | Expected \(L_p[1/2,\sqrt2]\) preprocessing and \(L_p[1/2,1/\sqrt2]\) per log | Pomerance, *Fast, rigorous factorization and discrete logarithm algorithms*, 1987, pp. 119–143. I read the publisher’s summary stating both constants; the original scan was not text-readable through the available tools. [Publisher](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/chapter/edited-volume/abs/pii/B9780123868701500149). |
| Prime-field NFS | Heuristic \(L_p[1/3,(64/9)^{1/3}]\) | I read Schirokauer’s §2.8, including the explicit random-smoothness assumption and conjectured complexity. [Schirokauer, 2008, pp. 406–407](https://library.slmath.org/books/Book44/files/12oliver.pdf). |
| \(\mathbb F_{p^n}\), \(p\le n^{o(n)}\) | Rigorous expected \(L_{p^n}[1/2,\sqrt2]\) | Lovorn Bender–Pomerance, 1998, pp. 221–232. I located the original scan but could not read its text; the stated range is explicitly recorded in Galbraith §15.5.3, p. 338, which I read. I have not independently checked sharper transition-range constants. [Original](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper115.pdf), [Galbraith](https://www.math.auckland.ac.nz/~sgal018/crypto-book/ch15.pdf). |
| Explicit extension families in fixed characteristic | Rigorous quasi-polynomial time | Granger–Kleinjung–Zumbrägel, Theorems 1–2. I read those statements and their model hypotheses; their all-extension conclusion there requires an additional conjecture. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1507.01495). |
| Every \(\mathbb F_{p^n}\) | Expected \((pn)^{2\log_2n+O(1)}\), unconditionally | Kleinjung–Wesolowski, *JAMS* 35 (2022), 581–624, Theorem 1.1. I read the introduction, model construction, and §3 overview, not the complete elimination proof. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.10668), [Published abstract](https://doi.org/10.1090/jams/985). |

I also read the relevant analysis in Garefalakis–Panario, *Math. Comp.* 70 (2001), 1253–1264: their generalized polynomial factor base preserves the classical asymptotic bound; their Waterloo and Coppersmith analyses remain heuristic. [Author-uploaded text](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220576694_The_index_calculus_method_using_non-smooth_polynomials).

My search found **no better rigorous general prime-field bound**, either for every prime or a density-one set of primes, unconditionally or under GRH alone. That is a search finding, not a theorem asserting that no such publication exists. GRH alone does not establish the smoothness and relation-supply assumptions in the cited NFS analysis.

For medium characteristic, KW’s exact bound is particularly informative. If \(Q=p^n\) and \(p=L_Q[\beta,c]\), with fixed \(0<\beta<1\), it gives
\[
\log T_{\rm KW}
=
\left(\frac{2c(1-\beta)}{\log2}+o(1)\right)
(\log Q)^\beta(\log\log Q)^{2-\beta}.
\]
This calculation follows directly from their theorem. It is commonly expressed as \(L_Q[\beta+o(1)]\); it does **not** give \(L_Q[\beta,C]\) for fixed \(C\). For \(\beta<2/5\), it already beats a finite-constant \(L_Q[2/5]\) bound.

The present transfer does not automatically cover extension fields:

- Setting \(N=p^n\) produces arithmetic modulo an integer, not \(\mathbb F_{p^n}\). For example, \((\mathbb Z/9\mathbb Z)^\times\) has order \(6\), whereas \(\mathbb F_9^\times\) has order \(8\).
- Integer ratios reduce into the prime subfield, so they cannot supply uniform extension-field targets.
- A number-field reduction onto \(\mathbb F_{p^n}\) needs a residue-degree-\(n\) prime, requiring absolute field degree at least \(n\). That can exceed the entire degree budget of this design.

An extension-field analogue would therefore require a new relation-supply and split-counting construction, probably over a polynomial or relative-field model. XK’s exact-dependency principle is portable; the hypotheses supplied here do not establish the required extension-field supply chain.
